      <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Who should replace Salesforce in 2026?</summary>
          <p>You should consider a Salesforce alternative if any of these apply: (1) you are a 5–50 person SMB or startup that does not need Salesforce's enterprise customization depth and cannot justify the TCO plus consultant spend — HubSpot, Pipedrive, or Freshsales are the natural fit; (2) implementation is stuck in Salesforce with a 3–6 month rollout and you need a working CRM in days, not months — HubSpot Free or Pipedrive Essential ship working pipelines in a week; (3) your team is support-led and needs unified support + CRM — Freshsales or Zoho work best; (4) you need sales-workflow visuals or combined project/sales workflows — Monday Sales CRM is the answer; (5) the total cost with Salesforce Sales Cloud plus administrator invoices are past your budget line — the five alternatives are typically 40–70% cheaper.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is HubSpot still the #1 Salesforce alternative?</summary>
          <p>Yes, for most SMB, mid-market, and growth teams. HubSpot combines a genuinely free CRM tier (up to 1M contacts), an intuitive interface sales teams adopt voluntarily, and a unified Marketing/Sales/Service platform with no custom code needed for 80% of companies. The full breakdown of pricing, enterprise customization, and automation is in the HubSpot vs Salesforce review.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which Salesforce alternative costs the least at 50 users?</summary>
          <p>At 50 users, Zoho CRM Premium (~$35/user/mo) and Pipedrive Professional (~$49/user/mo) are the lowest-priced Salesforce alternatives. HubSpot Professional at 50 seats is still ~40% lower than Salesforce Sales Cloud Enterprise. Freshsales Growth (~$29/user/mo) is the lowest price when combined with the entire Freshworks support suite. Salesforce Sales Cloud Enterprise at 50 users plus extras routinely crosses $50k/year; these alternatives match features at $15–30k/year.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>When should you STAY on Salesforce and NOT switch alternatives?</summary>
          <p>You should stay on Salesforce if any apply: (1) your company heavily depends on Apex custom code, CPQ, Billing, custom objects, or the 7,000+ AppExchange apps; (2) you need multi-org architecture, multi-subsidiary or complex global permission sets; (3) your RevOps team already has certified admins and architects and the org is mature; (4) you have Sales + Service + Marketing + Commerce clouds already paid and integrated; (5) compliance verticals require GovCloud or Shield; (6) enterprise scale above 1,000 employees. Migration costs outweigh savings in these cases.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

